Model Internals

    When you look internal into the model this is how it's going to look.
    Here I am taking "Llama-3.1-8b-Instruct" model as example. Below is how it's going to look internally.

            LlamaForCausalLM(
              (model): LlamaModel(
                (embed_tokens): Embedding(128256, 4096)
                (layers): ModuleList(
                  (0-31): 32 x LlamaDecoderLayer(
                    (self_attn): LlamaAttention(
                      (q_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=4096, bias=False)
                      (k_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=1024, bias=False)
                      (v_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=1024, bias=False)
                      (o_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=4096, bias=False)
                    )
                    (mlp): LlamaMLP(
                      (gate_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=14336, bias=False)
                      (up_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=14336, bias=False)
                      (down_proj): Linear4bit(in_features=14336, out_features=4096, bias=False)
                      (act_fn): SiLUActivation()
                    )
                    (input_layernorm): LlamaRMSNorm((4096,), eps=1e-05)
                    (post_attention_layernorm): LlamaRMSNorm((4096,), eps=1e-05)
                  )
                )
                (norm): LlamaRMSNorm((4096,), eps=1e-05)
                (rotary_emb): LlamaRotaryEmbedding()
              )
              (lm_head): Linear(in_features=4096, out_features=128256, bias=False)
            )

    This structure represents the whole Neural Network of a model. Let's understand one by one.
    Here is how we can look at this structure
            (model)
                > embed_tokens
                > layers
                    > self_attn
                        > q_proj
                        > k_proj
                        > v_proj
                        > o_proj
                    > mlp
                        > gate_proj
                        > up_proj
                        > down_proj
                        > act_fn
                    > input_layernorm
                    > post_attention_layernorm
                > norm
                > rotary_emb
            (lm_head)
    So first it's "embedding" and "32 layers" after that "llm_head" at the end.

    LlamaForCausalLM -> "Llama" + "CausalLM"
        "Llama" is just a company given name.
        "Causa LLM" is the name that HuggingFace gives to LLM which generate contents.

    (model)
        It's just represents the model that "CausalLM" contains

    (embed_tokens): Embedding(128256, 4096)
        The numbers in this is trying to represent the dimensionality of the layer.
        "128256" (Vocabulary Size) → Number of possible input tokens.
        "4096" (Embedding Dimension) → Length of the vector(array of numbers) representation for each token.

        What exactly Embedding is doing?
            >   It's just taking tokens of an input and it's turning into bunch of number which best represents it's
                understanding of what that token represents.
            >   It's a mixer that mixes every possible token into whole 4096 different number.

        For example:
            Hello world

            After tokenization:
            [9906, 1917]

            embedding layer simply performs a lookup:
                embedding[9906]   # 4096-dimensional vector
                embedding[1917]   # another 4096-dimensional vector

                Result:
                [
                  [0.15, -0.32, ..., 1.24],   # Hello
                  [-0.41, 0.88, ..., -0.55]   # world
                ]

        How many parameters does this layer have?
            Vocabulary × Embedding Dimension
            128256 × 4096 = 525,336,576 parameters
            If stored in FP16:
            525M × 2 bytes ≈ 1.05 GB (So the embedding layer alone occupies roughly 1 GB of model memory)

        This is called VECTOR EMBEDDING

    (rotary_emb):
        It's just a technique llama user called rotary embedding which is a particular way that makes sure words in a
        sequence, the order in which they appear is sort of effected.

    (layers):
        The 32 layers where all the action happens

    (lm_head): Linear(in_features=4096, out_features=128256, bias=False)
        It takes the final 4096-dimensional vector and predicts which of the 128,256 tokens is most likely to come
        next.

    |

    Now the major part let's understand the (layers)
    (layers):
        The 32 layers where all the action happens.

        Transformers are often describes as combining Encoder Layers with Decoder Layer but most of the latest LLM
        models only have Decoder layers as above example of Llama.

        So the Decoder in Llama architecture consists of
        > attention layer - This is the thing google invented
            >   There are just mixing or algorithms which designed to mix the layer above.
            >   But In particular what they are trying to do is, figure out what is it the layers before me that I
                should be paying attention too.
            >   And they learn over time what their weights are set too to determines how they incorporate all the
                information in the layer above.
            >   And they do it by learning 4 different things. Something called
                (q_proj) query -> Which is, "WHAT I AM LOOKING FOR"
                (k_proj) key -> Which is, "WHAT EACH ONE TRYING TO INFORM ME"
                (v_proj) value -> Which is, "WHAT IS INFORMATION IN EACH PARTS OF THE LIST"
                (o_proj) output -> Which is, "FINAL OUTPUT IN DOING ALL OF THESE"
            >   query, key, value, output which are 4 types of parameters that get learned by the self attention layer.
            >   Final Conclusion: It's all about figuring out what matters from the layers that came before me.
        > mlp - Multilayer Perceptron
            >   This is the core of any Neural Network
            >   (gate_proj) ->
                    Its something that's trying to figure out what matters with all of these dimensions.
                    How can I filter the stuff that matters. Part of this gate is also called as None Linearity.
            >   (up_proj) -> up projections
                    Is something that explodes out the number of dimensions.
                    In our case "Linear4bit(in_features=4096, out_features=14336, bias=False)"
                    It increased the dimensions from 4096 to 14336
            >   (down_proj) -> down projections
                    Linear4bit(in_features=14336, out_features=4096, bias=False)
                    It's convert the dimensions back to 4096 from 14336 that we have before.
            >   (act_fn) -> activation function
                >   In this entire layer the (act_fn) is more important.
                >   The activation function (act_fn) is important because it adds non-linearity to the layers output.
                    Without it, the MLP could only learn simple linear relationships and wouldn't be able to understand
                    complex patterns in language.
                >   All these layers are connected to geather and this act_fn add that tiny distortion into the output,
                    like something that messy with the output in someway it isn't just a linear combination is what we
                    called non linear combination.
                >   As soon as we do this.. each layer will be able to influence the final output in slightly different
                    way and suddenly all of these layers become meaningful
                >   LLama uses SiLU -> https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.13/generated/torch.nn.SiLU.html
                >   ReLU: We can also take this, https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.13/generated/torch.nn.ReLU.html
            >   This is Multilayer Perceptron in each of these 32 layers.
        > layernorm
            It's just a mathematical trickery to try and make sure the numbers behave them selves and don't get flying
            too much or too small. This is called normalizing the numbers


